How to Find Errors in Wireshark:

Method 1: Expert Info (The EASY way!)
Bottom-left corner of Wireshark.
Click on the "Expert Information" icon (the little circle with an exclamation mark).
OR go to: Analyze → Expert Information.
I'll see a window with color-coded categories:


🟩 Notes (Green)    = Normal, informational
🟨 Warnings (Yellow)= Something to check
🟥 Errors (Red)     = PROBLEMS!
WARNINGS (Yellow) - Things to Pay Attention To:

1️⃣ DNS Issues (Warning Level)
DNS response retransmission  | Protocol: DNS  | Count: 1
DNS query retransmission     | Protocol: DNS  | Count: 2
DNS query retransmission     | Protocol: mDNS | Count: 4
DNS response retransmission  | Protocol: mDNS | Count: 7
DNS response missing         | Protocol: mDNS | Count: 14

What this means:
My PC asked for DNS lookups, but had to ask again (retransmissions).
14 mDNS responses were missing (mDNS = Multicast DNS, used for local device discovery).
This suggests network congestion or WiFi issues.
What to check: My WiFi signal, network congestion, or router performance.

2️⃣ TCP Sequence Issues (Warning Level)

Out-of-order segment         | Count: 6
Previous segment(s) not captured | Count: 24
Connection reset (RST)       | Count: 76  ← ALARMING!
D-SACK Sequence              | Count: 211 ← BIG PROBLEM!
What these mean:

Warning	What It Means	Severity
Connection reset (RST)	Connections being abruptly closed	⚠️ HIGH
D-SACK Sequence	Duplicate Selective ACK - packets arriving out of order	⚠️ HIGH
Out-of-order	Packets not arriving in correct sequence	⚠️ MEDIUM
Previous segment not captured	Packet loss or capture start	ℹ️ LOW
76 RST packets = 76 connections were forcefully terminated.

211 D-SACK = Significant packet reordering.

3️⃣ TLS/QUIC Issues
Ignored Unknown Record        | Protocol: TLS | Count: 2
Failed to decrypt handshake   | Protocol: QUIC | Count: 607
QUIC = A newer protocol (faster than TCP).

607 decryption failures = QUIC traffic is encrypted; Wireshark can't read it.
🟩 NOTES (Green) - Interesting Observations:
Keep-Alive Traffic

TCP keep-alive segment   | Count: 209
ACK to TCP keep-alive    | Count: 202
Connection closing       | Count: 241 + 250
What this means: My PC is actively maintaining connections, keeping them alive, and 
properly closing them. This is NORMAL behavior.

Retransmissions
This frame is a (suspected) retransmission | Count: 25
25 retransmissions = Some packet loss, but not catastrophic.

Packet Length Exceeds MSS
This packet's length exceeds MSS | Count: 411
MSS = Maximum Segment Size.
What this means: My network card is using TSO (TCP Segmentation Offload) – 
the OS sends large packets and the network card splits them. This is NORMAL.

💬 CHAT (Blue) - Normal Operations:

Connection establish request (SYN)      | Count: 260
Connection establish acknowledge (SYN+ACK) | Count: 260
Connection finish (FIN)                 | Count: 491
What this means: My PC established 260 TCP connections and closed 491 connections.

Interesting: More FINs than SYNs means I closed connections that were already established (normal for web browsing).
